- op: discuss  # 8dcf
  at: '2026-08-17T22:57:09Z'  # 8dcf
  by: agent  # 8dcf
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 8dcf
  clock: 4  # 8dcf
  nonce: 8dcf  # 8dcf
  message: "PRIORITY CHANGED 2026-08-17, on owner authorisation, as part of the P0/P1 sweep. Recording the reasoning here because a re-rank that lives only in conversation is indistinguishable next session from drift.\n\nTHE GOVERNING MEASUREMENT. docs/measurements/0001-taint-flow-precision.md puts taint-flow precision at ~41% population-weighted across six real repositories. Per WI-sivuz's PRE-REGISTERED decision bands -- named before any interval was computed, so the interval cannot be used to move them -- that is the <50% band: \"precision is the headline problem and recall work should stop until it is understood.\"\n\nP1 -> P2, AND THIS ONE HAS A SECOND REASON THAT IS NOT ABOUT RANKING. Emitting the missing edges populates callees_at, which lets _use_site_terminates decide sites it currently treats as escapes, which produces MORE False verdicts -- and since PR #214 a barrier False earns `sanitized` and DROPS the flow from the claim's violation set. So fixing this can MANUFACTURE SUPPRESSION, into a reporting layer already at ~41% precision. That is the unsafe direction for a security tool, and it is an argument for doing it AFTER the precision mechanisms are understood, not before.\n\nTHE DEFECT IS UNCHANGED and re-reproduced at tip 2026-08-17: print(x), len(x) and undefined_fn(x) emit no edge while helper(x), os.remove(x) and obj.decode() do, in one run."  # 8dcf
- op: discuss  # 753b
  at: '2026-08-30T08:42:52Z'  # 753b
  by: agent  # 753b
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 753b
  clock: 5  # 753b
  nonce: 753b  # 753b
  message: "input() IS A MISSING TAINT SOURCE, NOT ONLY A MISSING SECTION-4 SUMMARY, and that raises this item's stakes. This item's own measured table already lists `input()  builtin  none`. What it does not say is what that edge would have carried: input() reads standard input, so it is the canonical untrusted-input source in Python, and with no edge there is no source -- a fixture doing `os.makedirs(input('name? '))` produces NO call edge for input at all (verified today, production analyze_python; the os.makedirs sink edge is emitted normally beside it).\n\nTHE TWO HALVES MAY CLOSE WITH ONE ROW, and it follows from this item's own root_cause. The permitting condition is BUILTIN_CONSTRUCTOR_NAMES, 'a set derived from the io_primitives catalogue'. builtins.input is ABSENT from python.yaml under every boundary -- python's ambiguous_names even carries the comment 'input # fileinput.input vs builtins.input (not IO boundary)', which is wrong on its face. So cataloguing builtins.input as ipc_recv may admit the edge as a side effect of the derivation, closing the recall hole and this item's arm for that name together. Filed as part of INV-muvis (unconditional stdin reads absent or declared fs_read across five languages).\n\nDO NOT TREAT THAT AS ESTABLISHED. It is an inference from this item's root_cause text, not a run -- if the bare-Name arm consults a different set, or consults it before the catalogue is loaded, the row will not admit the edge and the analyzer half stands on its own. Verify before claiming either half is closed."  # 753b
- op: discuss  # b44a
  at: '2026-08-30T08:52:30Z'  # b44a
  by: agent  # b44a
  actor: jgstern_agent  # b44a
  clock: 6  # b44a
  nonce: b44a  # b44a
  message: "REFUTED, BY THE CHECK THE PRECEDING MESSAGE ASKED FOR. That message hypothesised\nthat cataloguing builtins.input as ipc_recv might admit the missing edge as a\nside effect, because this item's root_cause describes BUILTIN_CONSTRUCTOR_NAMES\nas \"a set derived from the io_primitives catalogue\". It is not derived. Read in\ncode, py.py:568:\n\n    BUILTIN_CONSTRUCTOR_NAMES: frozenset[str] = frozenset({\"open\"})\n\nA hardcoded one-element literal. So a catalogue row for builtins.input will NOT\nadmit the edge, and the two halves do NOT close with one change: the row is\nneeded for the boundary, and the NAME is separately needed here. Recording the\nrefutation rather than quietly dropping the claim -- it was posted with an\nexplicit \"verify before claiming it\", and this is the verification coming back\nnegative.\n\nTHIS ALSO CORRECTS THIS ITEM'S OWN root_cause FIELD, which is the more useful\nhalf. The field says the permitting condition is \"this builtin is a catalogued\nI/O primitive\" and calls WI-mitul's fix \"added open() to the same table rather\nthan removing the coupling\". There is no coupling to remove: the table never\nconsulted the catalogue. WI-mitul added the string \"open\" to a literal, and every\nother builtin -- print, input, eval, len, getattr -- is outside it for the plain\nreason that nobody has typed the name. That makes this item CHEAPER than its\nroot_cause implies for any single name, and it also makes it a per-name allowlist\nthat will keep failing open, which is the shape the field was right to object to."  # b44a
- op: discuss  # 1034
  at: '2026-08-30T09:49:08Z'  # 1034
  by: agent  # 1034
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 1034
  clock: 7  # 1034
  nonce: '1034'  # 1034
  message: "SECOND LANGUAGE, SAME MECHANISM: HASKELL EMITS NO CALL EDGE FOR A BARE-NAME IO\nACTION EITHER. Measured today on a fixture, production analyze_haskell:\n\n    main = do\n      name <- getLine          -- NO call edge\n      contents <- getContents  -- NO call edge\n      writeFile name contents\n\nTwo edges come out of the module and neither is one of these. This item's own\nmeasured table is python's version of the same list (print/input/eval/len/\ngetattr emit nothing), so the defect is not py.py's allowlist alone -- it is\n\"an analyzer emits no call edge for a callee it cannot resolve in-project\",\nnow seen in two analyzers, which is the standing two-languages threshold for\ntreating something as a class rather than an instance.\n\nCONSEQUENCE, and it is why this matters more than a missing section-4 summary:\nINV-muvis catalogued builtins.input (python) and Prelude.{getLine,getChar,\ngetContents,readLn,interact} (haskell) as ipc_recv, which AUTO_SOURCE_LABEL_MAP\nmaps to untrusted_input. Those rows are correct and CANNOT FIRE, in either\nlanguage, because no edge reaches them. The canonical untrusted-input source in\nboth languages is invisible for this reason and not for a catalogue reason.\n\nRecorded here rather than filed separately because the mechanism and the remedy\nare this item's, not a new one's -- but note that this item's title, statement\nand root_cause are all python-scoped, so a reader looking for the haskell arm\nwill not find it by title. If the class is worth its own item, this is the\nevidence for splitting one out.\n\nFixture: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/vutav_reads_08302026/reach/hs/"  # 1034
